While I have achieved high marks in my Computer Science units that use Python, I still don't have experience with large Python projects and codebases. My attempt to make Root in Python is my largest personal project so far, and that codebase is *bad*. I also don't have a lot of experience with the key Python libraries used by data scientists, actual scientists and engineers.

Below I have listed some Python libraries I would like to get better at, and useful textbooks and educational resources. Everyone uses Python for everything so this is probably pretty important.

## Python Libraries
- [*NumPy*](https://numpy.org/learn/) 
- [*Pandas*](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/index.html)
- [*Matplotlib*](https://matplotlib.org/stable/users/index)
    - Also see [*Seaborn*](https://seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial.html)
    - For quick data apps: [*Streamlit*](https://docs.streamlit.io/)

## O'Reilly Digital Library
At the time of writing, I pay an exorbitant $79 AUD monthly subscription for the [O'Reilly](https://learning.oreilly.com/) learning platform. This includes access to the folllowing textbooks:
- *Fluent Python (2nd edition)*
- *Automate the Boring Stuff with Python (2nd edition)* (notably I also own a physical copy of this book)
- *Think Complexity* among other books by Allen B. Downey (also available for free)
- *Data Structures & Algorithms in Python*
